      "content": "Critics of extreme socioeconomic inequality often argue that raw test score differences persist even when controlling for income, attributing the variance to genetic potential and unmeasured environmental factors like community violence or neighborhood instability. Some researchers, particularly those skeptical of broad poverty explanations, point out that test scores often correlate with broader societal trends regardless of family income, suggesting that innate cognitive variation or cumulative environmental stress may play a more significant role than social injustice alone. Additionally, proponents of genetic-influenced theories claim that standardized tests implicitly measure non-cognitive traits like diligence and anxiety, which they argue may have heritable components and contribute to the observed disparities. To counter these, you must emphasize that while genetic and unmeasured environmental factors exist, massive longitudinal data overwhelmingly demonstrates that institutional pathways and resource access are the strongest predictors of test outcomes, not inherent group differences.",
